            <div xml:id="d95">

                <pb facs="0184" n="102" sameAs="frus:frus1911/pg102"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 611.3731/16.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The American Minister to
                        the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Habana</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1911-02-23"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">February 23, 1911</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 646.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: While confirming my telegram of the 17th
                        instant,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d95-f1"/> I have the honor to report that
                    after the Senate passed the bill to amend the customs tariff as follows: <table
                        cols="3" rows="2">
                        <row>
                            <cell>No. 293, sugar, raw</cell>
                            <cell role="num">$1.00</cell>
                            <cell>per 100 kilograms.</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>No. 294, sugar, refined</cell>
                            <cell role="num">.75</cell>
                            <cell>per 100 kilograms.</cell>
                        </row>
                    </table></p>
                <p rendition="#flush">the secretary of state requested me to try again to obtain an
                    answer to the inquiry contained in his note of January 2, to which I referred in
                    my dispatch No. 626 and several previous communications. No answer being
                    received from you, and the sugar convention at Brussels being unwilling to delay
                    action in regard to Cuban sugar beyond the 1st of next April, President Gómez
                    pressed this matter on the Chamber of Representatives and that body passed the
                    bill yesterday before the close of the session. The new law will go into effect
                    upon <pb facs="0185" n="103" xml:id="pg103"/> its publication in the Official
                    Gazette, and it is probable that the President will sign it and cause it to be
                    published at an early date.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John B. Jackson.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
